.TH "LOCALCI" "1" "May 2026" "LocalCI Manual"
.SH NAME
localci \- local post\-commit validation runner
.SH COMMAND HELP
For command options, run \f[CR]localci \-\-help\f[R] or
\f[CR]localci <command> \-\-help\f[R].
.SH Overview
LocalCI is an asynchronous post\-commit validation runner for local
development.
It fills the gap between Git hooks and remote CI: checks run locally,
but they run after the commit so the person or coding agent making the
change does not have to block on the full suite before moving on.
.PP
LocalCI is deliberately small.
It discovers mise tasks, queues them in a daemon, runs them against an
isolated clone, and makes the results available from the CLI and a local
web UI.
.SS What LocalCI is for
.IP \(bu 2
✓ \f[B]Run all checks even if you forget\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
You or your agent might forget to keep one of your test suites green.
LocalCI makes sure everything runs all the time.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
✓ \f[B]Browse results like GitHub Actions (but better)\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
Results, logs, and history are all available via one\-shot commands, a
web UI, and a terminal UI.
.RE
.SS What LocalCI is not for
.IP \(bu 2
✕ \f[B]GitHub Actions compatibility\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
LocalCI remains simple by sticking to running Mise tasks.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
✕ \f[B]Projects without Mise\f[R]
.RS 2
.PP
If a project cannot express its checks as Mise tasks, it is not a good
fit.
.RE
.SS How it works
.IP "1." 3
The daemon is running.
(\f[CR]localci start\f[R])
.IP "2." 3
A Git post\-commit hook calls
\f[CR]localci postcommit \-\-repo <repo> \-\-commit <commit>\f[R].
.IP "3." 3
The LocalCI daemon clones your project to a unique place and checks out
the commit.
.IP "4." 3
The daemon discovers every mise task whose task name starts with
\f[CR]localci:\f[R], including monorepo tasks addressed with mise\(cqs
\f[CR]//path:task\f[R] syntax, and puts them in a queue.
.IP "5." 3
One by one, the daemon runs each task.
.IP "6." 3
Results can be inspected with \f[CR]localci wait\f[R],
\f[CR]localci status\f[R], \f[CR]localci web\f[R], or
\f[CR]localci dash\f[R].
.PP
By default, LocalCI runs everything in isolated clones.
Use \f[CR]localci run \-\-no\-clone \-\-wait\f[R] when you intentionally
want a daemon\-managed run against your working tree.
.SH Getting Started
This guide gets a repository to the point where LocalCI runs checks
after each commit.
.SS Set up a repository
.SS Install LocalCI
LocalCI requires \c
.UR https://mise.en.dev
Mise
.UE \c
\&.
Install Mise first, then add LocalCI as a pinned GitHub release tool:
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][tools]\f[R]
\(dqgithub:irskep/localci\(dq = \(dq0.2.1\(dq
.EE
.IP
.EX
mise install
.EE
.SS Start the daemon
.IP
.EX
localci start
.EE
.SS Read docs in the terminal
.IP
.EX
localci docs
.EE
.PP
Use \f[CR]localci docs \-\-plain\f[R] when you want plain text for
scripts, pagers, or agents.
.SS Install the hook
From the repository root:
.IP
.EX
localci install\-hooks
.EE
.PP
After each commit, the hook enqueues checks and prints commands for
status and waiting.
.SS Define tasks
LocalCI executes all tasks with \f[CR]localci:\f[R] in their name, and
always executes \f[CR]localci:setup\f[R] first.
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][tasks.\(dq\f[R]localci:setup\(dq\f[B]]\f[R]
description = \(dqInstall dependencies for LocalCI runs\(dq
run = [
  \f[I]# \(aqmise trust\(aq is run automatically, so you can usually just install dependencies immediately\f[R]
  \(dqmise install\(dq,
]

\f[B][tasks.\(dq\f[R]localci:test\(dq\f[B]]\f[R]
description = \(dqgo test\(dq
run = \(dqgo test ./...\(dq
.EE
.SS Commit the setup
Commit the LocalCI task definitions so the post\-commit hook can enqueue
checks for the new revision:
.IP
.EX
git add mise.toml
git commit \-m \(dqConfigure LocalCI\(dq
.EE
.SS Run checks
.SS Follow the latest run
The normal workflow is post\-commit: the hook queues work after each
commit, and you inspect the daemon\(cqs latest run.
.PP
Wait in the terminal:
.IP
.EX
localci wait
.EE
.PP
Show a bounded status summary without waiting:
.IP
.EX
localci status
.EE
.PP
Open the same run in a browser or terminal UI:
.IP
.EX
localci web
localci dash
.EE
.PP
After \f[CR]localci postcommit\f[R] enqueues tasks, use the wait command
it prints:
.IP
.EX
localci wait \-\-repo <repo> \-\-commit <commit>
.EE
.PP
To inspect an existing no\-clone run:
.IP
.EX
localci wait \-\-commit \(aqHEAD*\(aq
.EE
.SS Run an ad hoc daemon check
Use \f[CR]run\f[R] when you explicitly want to queue a manual
daemon\-managed run outside the post\-commit path.
.IP
.EX
localci run \-\-task test \-\-wait
.EE
.PP
To test uncommitted changes through the daemon queue, use
\f[CR]\-\-no\-clone\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
localci run \-\-no\-clone \-\-task test \-\-wait
.EE
.PP
No\-clone runs are labeled with a trailing \f[CR]*\f[R].
They intentionally see unstaged files and local edits.
.PP
Use \f[CR]invoke\f[R] only when you want to run checks directly in the
current terminal instead of through the daemon.
.SS Inspect results
.SS Check status
Show the latest run for the current repository:
.IP
.EX
localci status
.EE
.PP
Show one task:
.IP
.EX
localci status \-\-task noisy\-fail
.EE
.PP
Show a specific run:
.IP
.EX
localci status \-\-commit 6bd8d389f3f550da3fccb11bac380d59268fe320
.EE
.PP
Non\-interactive commands print bounded summaries.
They do not dump full logs.
.SS Find artifact paths
Print artifact paths for the latest run:
.IP
.EX
localci artifacts
.EE
.PP
Print the primary artifact for failed tasks:
.IP
.EX
localci artifacts \-\-failed \-\-primary
.EE
.PP
For shell scripts and agents, print only paths:
.IP
.EX
localci artifacts \-\-task noisy\-fail \-\-primary \-\-paths\-only
.EE
.PP
Then read the file directly:
.IP
.EX
cat \(dq$(localci artifacts \-\-task noisy\-fail \-\-primary \-\-paths\-only)\(dq
.EE
.SS Browse history
Show recent runs:
.IP
.EX
localci history
.EE
.PP
Show recent results for a task across commits:
.IP
.EX
localci history \-\-task noisy\-fail
.EE
.PP
Show recent failed runs:
.IP
.EX
localci history \-\-failed
.EE
.SS Open UIs
.SS Open the web UI
Open the latest run for the current repository:
.IP
.EX
localci web
.EE
.PP
Open a specific task:
.IP
.EX
localci web \-\-task noisy\-fail
.EE
.PP
Task artifact pages show full artifact paths and include copy/open
actions.
.SS Use the terminal UI
Open the all\-repos dashboard:
.IP
.EX
localci dash
.EE
.PP
Open a specific task:
.IP
.EX
localci dash \-\-task noisy\-fail
.EE
.PP
The terminal UI supports the same run, task, retry, cancel, and artifact
views as the web UI.
On artifact views, use \f[CR]e\f[R] to edit the selected artifact with
\f[CR]$VISUAL\f[R] or \f[CR]$EDITOR\f[R], \f[CR]o\f[R] to open it with
the platform opener, and \f[CR]f\f[R] on macOS to reveal it in Finder
through the daemon.
.SS Manage queued work
.SS Cancel work
Cancel the active task:
.IP
.EX
localci cancel
.EE
.PP
Cancel a specific task:
.IP
.EX
localci cancel \-\-commit HEAD \-\-task test
.EE
.SH Tasks
LocalCI runs checks that are defined as Mise tasks.
A task is eligible when its task name starts with \f[CR]localci:\f[R].
.PP
LocalCI discovers tasks with:
.IP
.EX
mise tasks \-\-json \-\-all
.EE
.PP
It then runs each matching task with:
.IP
.EX
mise run <task\-name>
.EE
.SS Defining tasks
.SS TOML tasks
Use TOML tasks when a check is short and belongs near the rest of your
Mise configuration.
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][tasks.\(dq\f[R]localci:test\(dq\f[B]]\f[R]
description = \(dqRun tests\(dq
run = \(dqgo test ./...\(dq
.EE
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][tasks.\(dq\f[R]localci:fmt\(dq\f[B]]\f[R]
description = \(dqFormat Go files\(dq
run = \(dqgofmt \-w ./cmd ./internal\(dq
.EE
.PP
Mise documents this format in \c
.UR https://mise.en.dev/tasks/toml-tasks.html
TOML tasks
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Nested \f[CR]mise.toml\f[R] files
TOML tasks also work from nested Mise config roots.
Tell Mise which child directories are config roots from the repo\-level
\f[CR]mise.toml\f[R]:
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
experimental_monorepo_root = true

\f[B][monorepo]\f[R]
config_roots = [\(dqweb\(dq, \(dqdocs\(dq]
.EE
.PP
Then define LocalCI tasks inside those child configs:
.PP
File: \f[CR]web/mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][tasks.\(dq\f[R]localci:test\(dq\f[B]]\f[R]
description = \(dqRun Vue unit tests\(dq
run = \(dqpnpm exec vitest \-\-run\(dq
.EE
.PP
File: \f[CR]docs/mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][tasks.\(dq\f[R]localci:build\(dq\f[B]]\f[R]
description = \(dqBuild documentation\(dq
run = \(dqzensical build \-\-strict\(dq
.EE
.PP
LocalCI uses Mise\(cqs task list, so nested tasks keep their path
prefix.
In the UI, LocalCI removes the \f[CR]localci:\f[R] namespace from task
labels: a root task like \f[CR]localci:test\f[R] shows as
\f[CR]test\f[R], and a nested task like \f[CR]//web:localci:test\f[R]
shows as \f[CR]//web:test\f[R].
.PP
Mise documents the path syntax in \c
.UR https://mise.jdx.dev/tasks/monorepo.html
monorepo tasks
.UE \c
\&.
.SS File tasks
Use file tasks when a check is easier to maintain as a script.
.IP
.EX
mkdir \-p mise\-tasks/localci
.EE
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise\-tasks/localci/test\f[R]
.IP
.EX
go test ./...
.EE
.IP
.EX
chmod +x mise\-tasks/localci/test
.EE
.PP
Mise documents this format in \c
.UR https://mise.en.dev/tasks/file-tasks.html
file tasks
.UE \c
\&.
.SS File tasks in monorepos
File tasks work the same way inside nested config roots.
For example, this file:
.PP
File: \f[CR]web/mise\-tasks/localci/test\f[R]
.IP
.EX
pnpm exec vitest \-\-run
.EE
.PP
is exposed by Mise as \f[CR]//web:localci:test\f[R], and LocalCI shows
it in the UI as \f[CR]//web:test\f[R].
.SS Setup tasks
Use a root \f[CR]localci:setup\f[R] task for dependency installation
shared by all checks.
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise.toml\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B][tasks.\(dq\f[R]localci:setup\(dq\f[B]]\f[R]
description = \(dqInstall dependencies for LocalCI runs\(dq
run = \(dqmise install\(dq
.EE
.PP
LocalCI runs \f[CR]localci:setup\f[R] first when it exists.
It is setup for the run, not an ordinary validation check.
.PP
Only the root setup task is used.
LocalCI ignores setup tasks from nested config roots, such as
\f[CR]//web:localci:setup\f[R].
If you need package\-specific setup, use \c
.UR https://mise.en.dev/tasks/architecture.html#task-dependency-system
task dependencies
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Task execution environment
Tasks run either in shallow clones of a specific commit, or on your
working directory when you pass \f[CR]\-\-no\-clone\f[R].
Runs with \f[CR]\-\-no\-clone\f[R] are marked with an asterisk
\f[CR]*\f[R] in the UI because their state won\(cqt match what\(cqs in
git.
.SS Environment variables
Every task receives these environment variables:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(35.0n) lw(35.0n).
T{
Variable
T}@T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR\f[R]
T}@T{
Directory for artifacts from this task attempt.
T}
T{
\f[CR]LOCALCI_TASK_CACHE_DIR\f[R]
T}@T{
Cache directory for this task.
T}
T{
\f[CR]LOCALCI_CACHE_DIR\f[R]
T}@T{
Cache directory shared by LocalCI tasks.
T}
.TE
.PP
Write reports and inspectable artifacts under
\f[CR]LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR\f[R].
LocalCI exposes those files in the web and terminal UIs.
.SS Task output
LocalCI captures each task\(cqs stdout and stderr into
\f[CR]combined.log\f[R].
For ordinary checks, let the task print normally:
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise\-tasks/localci/test\f[R]
.IP
.EX
go test ./...
.EE
.PP
For tasks with multiple outputs, write them under
\f[CR]LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR\f[R]:
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise\-tasks/localci/test\-with\-coverage\f[R]
.IP
.EX
mkdir \-p \(dq$LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR/coverage\(dq
pytest \-\-cov \-\-cov\-report=html:\(dq$LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR/coverage\(dq
.EE
.PP
Here\(oqs how you might configure a browser testing tool like Playwright
to put its filesystem output in the right place:
.PP
File: \f[CR]playwright.config.ts\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] { defineConfig } \f[B]from\f[R] \(aq\(atplaywright/test\(aq

\f[B]export\f[R] \f[B]default\f[R] defineConfig({
  outputDir: process.env.LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR
    ? \(ga${process.env.LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR}/playwright\-artifacts\(ga
    : \(aqtest\-results\(aq,
  reporter: [
    [\(aqlist\(aq],
    [
      \(aqhtml\(aq,
      {
        outputFolder: process.env.LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR
          ? \(ga${process.env.LOCALCI_TASK_OUTPUT_DIR}/playwright\-report\(ga
          : \(aqplaywright\-report\(aq,
        open: \(aqnever\(aq,
      },
    ],
  ],
})
.EE
.PP
File: \f[CR]mise\-tasks/localci/playwright\f[R]
.IP
.EX
pnpm exec playwright test
.EE
